TASK 2 REPORT — RESTAURANT RECOMMENDATION SYSTEM
Intern Name: Aniket Krushna Satav
Role: Machine Learning Intern
Organization: Cognifyz Technologies

1. INTRODUCTION
A restaurant recommendation system can help users discover restaurants that match their preferences. Recommendation systems can use information about restaurants such as cuisine, location, pricing, ratings, and available services to identify suitable options.
As part of the Cognifyz Technologies Machine Learning Internship, this task focuses on developing a content-based restaurant recommendation system. The system accepts user preferences and recommends restaurants by filtering suitable restaurants and ranking them according to content similarity and restaurant rating.
The Cognifyz task requires defining recommendation criteria such as cuisine and price range, implementing a content-based filtering approach, and testing the recommendation system using sample user preferences.

2. OBJECTIVE
The main objectives of this task are:
• To develop a restaurant recommendation system based on user preferences.
• To preprocess restaurant data required for recommendation.
• To define recommendation criteria such as city, cuisine, and price range.
• To implement content-based filtering.
• To use TF-IDF to represent restaurant content.
• To calculate similarity using cosine similarity.
• To rank restaurants according to their suitability for the user.
• To test the system using different sample user preferences.
• To evaluate whether the generated recommendations satisfy the requested preferences.

3. DATASET DESCRIPTION
The restaurant dataset provided for the internship contains 9,551 restaurant records and 21 attributes.
The dataset contains restaurant information including Restaurant ID, Restaurant Name, Country Code, City, Address, Locality, Longitude, Latitude, Cuisines, Average Cost for two, Currency, Has Table booking, Has Online delivery, Is delivering now, Switch to order menu, Price range, Aggregate rating, Rating color, Rating text, and Votes.
For the recommendation task, restaurant attributes related to location, cuisine, price, rating, and services were used to create the recommendation system.

4. DATA PREPARATION
A separate recommendation dataset was created from the cleaned restaurant dataset. The selected information included Restaurant Name, City, Locality, Cuisines, Price range, Aggregate rating, Has Online delivery, and Has Table booking.
Text-based features such as City, Locality, and Cuisines were cleaned by handling missing values, converting values to strings, removing unnecessary spaces, and normalizing text to lowercase.
The recommendation system retains the complete restaurant dataset because restaurants marked as "Not rated" may still be valid restaurant choices for recommendation.

5. RECOMMENDATION FEATURES
The system uses the following information:
• City
• Cuisine
• Locality
• Price range
• Aggregate rating
• Online delivery availability
• Table booking availability
These attributes provide information that can be used to match restaurants with user preferences.

6. RECOMMENDATION CRITERIA
The system accepts the following user preferences:
• City
• Cuisine
• Price range
• Minimum rating
• Online delivery
The user can therefore specify the type of restaurant they want and the constraints they consider important.

7. CONTENT-BASED FILTERING APPROACH
A content-based filtering approach was selected for the recommendation system. In this approach, recommendations are generated using the characteristics of the restaurants rather than historical interactions between users and restaurants.
The system first filters restaurants according to explicit user preferences. The remaining restaurants are then ranked according to their similarity to the user's requested cuisine and city.

The recommendation workflow is:

User Preferences
        ↓
Preference-Based Filtering
        ↓
Matching Restaurants
        ↓
TF-IDF Representation
        ↓
Cosine Similarity
        ↓
Similarity and Rating-Based Ranking
        ↓
Top N Recommendations

8. CONTENT REPRESENTATION
A combined content feature was created using the restaurant's cuisine, locality, and city information.
For example, information such as:
North Indian, Chinese
Connaught Place
New Delhi
is combined into a text representation that can be processed using TF-IDF.
This representation allows restaurants with similar cuisine and location-related characteristics to obtain higher similarity scores.

9. TF-IDF VECTORIZATION
TF-IDF, or Term Frequency-Inverse Document Frequency, was used to convert restaurant content into numerical vectors.
The TF-IDF representation captures the importance of terms within the restaurant descriptions and provides a numerical representation that can be compared between restaurants and user preferences.
The same TF-IDF representation was used to convert the user's cuisine and city preferences into a numerical vector.

10. COSINE SIMILARITY
Cosine similarity was used to measure the similarity between the user preference vector and restaurant content vectors.
A higher cosine similarity indicates that the restaurant's content representation is more similar to the user's specified cuisine and location preferences.
The similarity score was used as an important component of the final recommendation ranking.

11. USER PREFERENCE FILTERING
Before calculating similarity, restaurants were filtered according to the user's selected constraints.
The filtering process included:
• City
• Cuisine
• Price range
• Minimum rating
• Online delivery preference
Applying these filters ensures that restaurants returned by the system satisfy the specified requirements before ranking is performed.

12. RECOMMENDATION FUNCTION
A reusable function named recommend_restaurants() was implemented to generate recommendations.

The function accepts:
• city
• cuisine
• price_range
• min_rating
• online_delivery
• top_n

The function performs input validation, filters restaurants, creates the user preference representation, calculates cosine similarity, calculates ranking scores, sorts the restaurants, and returns the requested number of recommendations.
Input validation was included to prevent invalid values such as an empty city, empty cuisine, an invalid price range, or a rating outside the allowed range.

13. RATING SCORE
In addition to similarity, restaurant rating was included in the final ranking process.
The restaurant rating was normalized to a 0–1 scale using:

Rating Score = Aggregate Rating / 5

This makes the rating comparable with the cosine similarity score.

14. FINAL RANKING SCORE
The final ranking score was calculated as:

Final Score = 0.7 × Similarity Score + 0.3 × Rating Score

The similarity component receives the larger weight so that recommendations remain primarily aligned with the user's content preferences, while restaurant rating contributes as a secondary quality factor.
The resulting restaurants are sorted in descending order of Final Score and the top N restaurants are returned.

15. SAMPLE USER PREFERENCE
The recommendation system was tested using sample user preferences such as:

City: New Delhi
Cuisine: North Indian
Price Range: 2
Minimum Rating: 4.0
Online Delivery: Yes
Number of Recommendations: 10

The function filters the restaurant dataset according to these preferences and returns a ranked list of suitable restaurants.

16. SAMPLE RECOMMENDATION RESULTS
The recommendation output contains information such as:
