17. TESTING WITH MULTIPLE USER PREFERENCES
The recommendation system was tested using multiple combinations of user preferences to confirm that the recommendation logic works for different situations.

Example test scenarios included:
• North Indian cuisine in New Delhi with a moderate price range and high minimum rating.
• Chinese cuisine in Gurgaon with online delivery.
• Italian cuisine in New Delhi with a higher price range and optional delivery preference.
• Highly restrictive preferences to test the no-result condition.
• Relaxed preferences to observe how increasing the available restaurant pool affects the recommendations.

Testing multiple scenarios helps demonstrate that the recommendation system is not limited to a single predefined input.

18. RECOMMENDATION VALIDATION
The recommendations were validated against the user's selected criteria.
The validation process checks whether returned restaurants satisfy:
• Requested city
• Requested cuisine
• Selected price range
• Minimum rating requirement
• Online delivery requirement when specified

This validation provides a direct check that the recommendation function respects the hard constraints provided by the user.

19. HANDLING NO-RESULT CASES
The system was designed to handle situations where no restaurants satisfy all selected preferences.
Instead of producing an error, the system returns an empty result and displays a message explaining that no restaurants match the selected preferences.
This behavior improves the robustness and usability of the recommendation system.

20. SIMILARITY ANALYSIS
Cosine similarity scores were analyzed to understand the ranking of recommended restaurants.
Restaurants with higher similarity scores are more closely aligned with the textual content of the user's cuisine and location preferences.
The similarity score represents content similarity and should not be interpreted as a probability that a user will like a particular restaurant.

21. RECOMMENDATION QUALITY EVALUATION
The recommendation system was evaluated using multiple sample user preference scenarios.
The returned restaurants were checked to determine whether they satisfied the selected constraints and whether the ranking reflected the similarity and rating scores.
The recommendation results were also examined using average similarity scores and the ordering of the returned restaurants.

22. VISUALIZATION
A visualization was created to show the similarity scores of the recommended restaurants.
The visualization provides a direct comparison of recommendation strength across the returned restaurants.

Figure: Top Restaurant Recommendations by Similarity / Final Score

23. RESULTS
The task successfully implemented a content-based restaurant recommendation system.
The complete workflow includes:
• Data preparation
• Text normalization
• User preference handling
• Preference-based filtering
• TF-IDF vectorization
• Cosine similarity
• Rating normalization
• Final ranking
• Top N recommendation generation
• Recommendation validation
• Multiple test scenarios

The system can generate recommendations dynamically according to the user's selected preferences.

24. KEY FINDINGS
The recommendation system demonstrates that available restaurant attributes can be used to build an explainable content-based recommendation approach.
Cuisine and location information provide the main content representation for similarity-based ranking, while price range, minimum rating, and online delivery act as explicit user constraints.
Combining similarity with restaurant rating provides a ranking mechanism that considers both user preference alignment and restaurant quality.
The testing process also demonstrates that the system can handle both normal and highly restrictive user requests.

25. LIMITATIONS
• The provided dataset does not contain historical user-restaurant interaction data such as clicks, bookings, purchases, or personalized user ratings.
• Because actual user feedback data is unavailable, standard recommendation metrics such as precision@K, recall@K, or NDCG cannot be reliably calculated from the provided dataset alone.
• The content-based similarity is based primarily on cuisine and location-related text.
• Price range, minimum rating, and online delivery are applied as filtering constraints rather than being fully integrated into the TF-IDF similarity representation.
• The ranking weights of 0.7 for similarity and 0.3 for rating are design choices used in this implementation.
• Recommendations therefore represent content and preference matching rather than guaranteed user satisfaction.

26. CONCLUSION
A content-based restaurant recommendation system was developed using the restaurant dataset provided for the Cognifyz Technologies Machine Learning Internship.
The system accepts user preferences such as city, cuisine, price range, minimum rating, and online delivery. Restaurants are first filtered using these preferences, after which the remaining restaurants are represented using TF-IDF and compared with the user's preferences using cosine similarity.
A rating score was also calculated and combined with similarity to produce a final ranking score. The system returns the top requested number of restaurants based on the resulting ranking.
Multiple sample user preference combinations were tested, including normal requests, optional preferences, restrictive requests, and no-result cases. The results were validated against the specified constraints.
Overall, this task provided practical experience in content-based recommendation, text feature representation, TF-IDF, cosine similarity, filtering, ranking, testing, validation, and recommendation-system limitations.

27. TECHNOLOGIES USED
Python, Pandas, NumPy, Matplotlib, Seaborn, Scikit-learn, Jupyter Notebook.

